  <h1 class="page-h1">Quitclaim Deed</h1>

  <div class="answer-block">
    <p><strong>A quitclaim deed is a legal document that transfers whatever property interest the grantor currently holds to the grantee — with no warranty that the title is clear or that the grantor owns the property free and clear.</strong> It is the standard instrument for transferring property between people who already trust each other: spouses dividing assets in a divorce, parents deeding a home to a child, an owner moving property into an LLC or revocable trust.</p>
    <p><em>If you already know you need a quitclaim deed, <a href="/quitclaim-deed-form/">go straight to the free generator →</a></em></p>
  </div>

  <Disclaimer />

  <h2>What a Quitclaim Deed Does (and What It Doesn't)</h2>
  <p>A quitclaim deed does one thing: it transfers title. The grantor — the person currently on the deed — signs over whatever ownership interest they hold. The grantee receives exactly that interest. No more, no less.</p>
  <p>What a quitclaim deed does <strong>not</strong> do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not clear a mortgage. If there is a loan on the property, the grantor remains on that loan after signing. The lender is not a party to the deed.</li>
    <li>It does not guarantee the title is clean. If someone else has a competing claim to the property, the grantee inherits that problem along with the deed.</li>
    <li>It does not transfer tax obligations, HOA responsibilities, or easements — those run with the land and are unaffected by a change in title.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The absence of a title guarantee is the feature, not a flaw. Between people who already know the ownership situation — a married couple, a parent and child, a business owner and their LLC — there is no need for the grantor to make formal title promises. The quitclaim structure is deliberately lightweight because the parties already trust each other's claim.</p>

  <h2>When to Use a Quitclaim Deed</h2>
  <p>Quitclaim deeds are the right instrument in specific situations. Outside those situations, a warranty deed or another instrument is usually more appropriate.</p>
  <ComparisonTable headers={whenToUseHeaders} rows={whenToUseRows} />

  <h3>The Divorce Case</h3>
  <p>The most common real-world use of a quitclaim deed is property division in a divorce. One spouse signs their interest over to the other. That deed handles the title.</p>
  <p>The mortgage is a separate matter entirely. If both names are on the loan — or if the departing spouse's name is on it — the deed does not change that. The bank was not a party to the deed. The departing spouse remains legally obligated on the mortgage until the remaining spouse refinances in their name alone, the property is sold, or the lender formally releases them. A divorce decree does not override this.</p>
  <p><a href="/quitclaim-deed/for-divorce/">Full explanation of quitclaim deeds in divorce →</a></p>

  <Callout type="info" title="California Parent-to-Child Transfers: Prop 19 Applies">
    <p>California operates under different rules for parent-to-child property transfers following Proposition 19 (effective February 16, 2021). Under the old law, parents could transfer a primary residence to a child with no reassessment for property tax purposes. Under Prop 19, the exclusion only applies if the child occupies the transferred property as their primary residence within one year — and the exclusion is capped. Using a quitclaim deed template that predates 2021 can result in a full property tax reassessment to current market value.</p>
    <p><a href="/quitclaim-deed/california/parents-to-children/">California parent-to-child quitclaim deed — Prop 19 requirements →</a></p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Quitclaim Deed vs. Warranty Deed</h2>
  <p>The most important comparison in real estate deed types:</p>
  <ComparisonTable headers={deedComparisonHeaders} rows={deedComparisonRows} />
  <p>The difference in a sentence: a warranty deed is a promise about the property's ownership history; a quitclaim deed is a release of whatever interest the grantor currently holds.</p>
  <p><a href="/quitclaim-deed-vs-warranty-deed/">Detailed comparison: quitclaim deed vs. warranty deed →</a></p>

  <h2>What a Quitclaim Deed Must Include</h2>
  <p>Every state has its own property code, and every state has its own requirements for deed execution. But the core elements are consistent across all 50 states:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Grantor's full legal name.</strong> The name must match exactly how it appears on the current title.</li>
    <li><strong>Grantee's full legal name and mailing address.</strong> This becomes the grantee's name on the public record.</li>
    <li><strong>Property legal description.</strong> Not the street address — the formal recorded description from the existing deed or county property records.</li>
    <li><strong>Consideration.</strong> The stated value exchanged. For family transfers, "$1 and other valuable consideration" is legally sufficient in most states.</li>
    <li><strong>Vesting.</strong> How the grantee will hold title: sole ownership, joint tenancy with right of survivorship, tenancy in common, or tenancy by the entireties (where available).</li>
    <li><strong>Grantor's notarized signature.</strong> Required in all 50 states. Some states also require witnesses (Florida: 2 witnesses; Tennessee: 2 witnesses; Georgia: 1 witness).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The Recording Process</h2>
  <p>Signing the deed is step one. The transfer is not legally effective against third parties until the deed is recorded.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Sign before a notary.</strong> The grantor signs the deed in the notary's presence. Do not pre-sign — the notary must witness the signing.</li>
    <li><strong>Record with the county recorder.</strong> File the original deed with the recorder's office in the county where the property is located.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay the recording fee.</strong> Typically $10–$30 for the first page, plus $5–$15 per additional page.</li>
    <li><strong>File any required accompanying documents.</strong> California requires a Preliminary Change of Ownership Report (PCOR) at recording.</li>
    <li><strong>Notify relevant parties.</strong> Inform your homeowner's insurance company and your mortgage lender.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Typical timeline:</strong> 30 minutes to generate the deed, 15–30 minutes at the notary, under one hour at the county recorder's office.</p>

  <h2>State-by-State Requirements at a Glance</h2>
  <p>There is no federal standard for quitclaim deeds. Each state sets its own rules for execution, recording, and transfer taxes.</p>
  <ComparisonTable headers={stateTableHeaders} rows={stateTableRows} />

